<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Duchy of Saxe-Weissenfels</h1><p class="st-meta">1656 CE – 1746 CE · capital Weissenfels</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Albertine Saxon duchy on the Saale whose lavish court employed musicians and gave Bach an honorary title.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After Elector <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-george-i-elector-of-saxony">John George I</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> died in 1656, his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augustus-duke-of-saxe-weissenfels">Augustus</a>, already administrator of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/archbishopric-of-magdeburg">Archbishopric of Magdeburg</a>, received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weissenfels">Weissenfels</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/querfurt">Querfurt</a> and other lands under his father's will, founding the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxe-weissenfels">Saxe-Weissenfels</a>. Augustus built the large <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neu-augustusburg">Neu-Augustusburg</a> palace at Weissenfels.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The duchy was Lutheran and depended on the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/electorate-of-saxony">Electorate of Saxony</a> in foreign affairs. Its court was a centre of music and opera: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-frideric-handel">Handel</a>'s father was surgeon to the duke, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-sebastian-bach">Johann Sebastian Bach</a> held the honorary title of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kapellmeister">Kapellmeister</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weissenfels">Weissenfels</a> from 1729. Lavish court spending left the state deeply in debt.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-adolf-ii-duke-of-saxe-weissenfels">Johann Adolf II</a>, the last duke and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> field marshal, died in 1746, and the duchy reverted to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/electorate-of-saxony">Electorate of Saxony</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weissenfels">Weissenfels</a> passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> in 1815 and is now in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony-anhalt">Saxony-Anhalt</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/duchy-of-saxe-weissenfels.jpg" alt="Duchy of Saxe-Weissenfels" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Concord · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Schloss_Neu-Augustusburg_Ostseite.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1656 CE – 1746 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Weissenfels</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Augustus, Johann Adolf I, Johann Adolf II</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Sachsen-Weißenfels</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1701&amp;focus=duchy-of-saxe-weissenfels" aria-label="Duchy of Saxe-Weissenfels on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4952 404 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Duchy of Saxe-Weissenfels lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5272 660L5312 664"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5312 664h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5272 660h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5272 660h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Weissenfels. <a href="/atlas?year=1701&amp;focus=duchy-of-saxe-weissenfels">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
